crusader kings makes me feel like a  smooth brained idiot  but honestly that's one of the reasons  why even in its most confusing and  convoluted moments  i still enjoy my time as a virtual ruler  recently i got some time with a preview  build of crusader kings 3 and i want to  share what i found from a couple days of  playing  there are a number of new additions in  the third iteration and a plethora of  quality of life changes  and i'll tell you this all through the  experiences of being a viking lord  ruler of the holy roman empire and  accidentally getting my  leg chopped off by a court physician now  like i said earlier these games quite  often make me feel like a dum-dum  many of the paradox franchises from  crusader kings to hearts of iron are  packed with complex mechanics  and concepts that while cleverly tied to  their historical setting  can be confusing the first thing that  struck me about crusader kings 3 was the  improvements to getting players  acclimated  while i expected my first hours to be  getting caught up in a web of menus and  trying to get my head around abstract  kingdom management its introduction was  worlds more accessible and  understandable than previous games have  been  while you're still able to jump in at  the deep end and start your lineage as  you see fit  ck3 also gives you the option to start  in the shallows  the fleshed-out tutorial following the  rise of the kingdom of ireland does a  great job of introducing new mechanics  to seasoned players  as well as serve as a good starting  point for newly crowned kings and queens  no more going to youtube to figure out  just what the hell a casus belly is  this used tab system now puts  information that you used to have to  hunt for at the forefront of your  interface  collating all the important and often  obscure details somewhere that is more  immediately apparent  and available at the click of a button  the guidance the game gives you is much  more reactive  keeping suggestions pertinent to your  current situation while also giving you  tips  on how to drive your kingdom forward  while experienced players may choose to  ignore this  with my moderate exposure to the series  it was extremely welcome to have what  felt like a virtual assistant  keeping tabs on all of the dozens of  whirling cogs driving events forward  [Music]  the most interesting aspects of how ck3  presents information to the player  however is how  tool tips flow and overlap with each  other almost like a teacher guiding you  through a lesson  now subjects of importance or concepts  that may need some explanation are  presented as a summary window containing  the exact surface level information you  need  if within that summary there are other  concepts you need to know more about or  there are connected mechanics they touch  upon  you can lock these guiding windows into  a cascade of information  that is being drip fed to you all at  your own pace and  to a depth of detail that you feel  comfortable with this method of peering  into the inner workings of the game is  in my opinion  a big step forward in how the franchise  teaches its players  instead of getting info dumps you are  able to see exactly how each idea and  option relates to each other  allowing you to go as deep or as  detailed as you need in order to address  the particular problem or make a  decision  now this accessibility has not dulled  the high level intrigue or complexity  that grand strategy players gravitate  towards  if anything there is much more of it now  it's just that little bit easier for  power-hungry rulers to seek their teeth  into elaborate schemes now i still got  lost and confused from time to time but  it was nice to at least be given the  opportunity to keep track of each moving  part  and have a path to figure out how to dig  my way out of trouble  battles have also grown to become much  more alive dynamic and understandable  also featuring the addition of knights  that exist as characters within the  lands that you rule and  fight alongside your peasant levy as  well as your other more well trained  units  they are able to improve the strength of  your force to a huge degree with their  own prowess skill  fighting against enemy knight characters  and potentially turning a losing battle  in your favor  or using their commander-specific skills  to increase your odds of victory  in the right moment they can even  prevent you from losing a battle that  would have cost you a whole war  having these elite units act like heroes  can not only make a huge difference to  your victories  if managed properly but the ability to  interact with them outside of the combat  encounters makes your military feel like  much more of a living entity  rather than simply a number of men to  call upon for an invasion  or a holy war many other elements have  also been altered to make you feel  closer to your troops  and for them to have more impact on your  playthrough as a whole  religions and faiths can alter depending  on their sizes and influence  for example a religion spanning  continents is no longer able to simply  stagnate  but its size and how fervorous its  worshippers are can make it more  susceptible to heresies  which then prevents further challenges  as well as options to quell uprisings or  convert yourself  smaller religions can also influence and  spread much easier now  which can make petty kingdoms with  different faiths cause problems for  larger empires  and while your faith is one of the  backbones to your kingdom the way  technology is tied to your culture and  how those mix  also provides more options as your  campaign progresses  while you're able to alter your  interests between the technology that is  available during your rule  being exposed to other cultures through  marriage or alliances  can also bring with it the inventions  discoveries and tech from the wider  corners of the world but the main thing  that really drew me into crusader kings  3 is that increased focus on the rpg  elements of building your character and  your dynasty  the actions that you take continue to  influence how your ruler develops  and you also have the ability to develop  your characteristics through the use of  a skill tree  giving you the opportunity to place more  focus on specific abilities  stats and perks these can then open more  options when it comes to random events  that will appear during your reign  and whether you want to create a raiding  viking lord and build your way through  military trees  or craft an infamous torturer king free  of the stresses or debuffs that go along  with burning some poor heretic at the  stake  now there are even more options  available to you visually the biggest  difference is that not only does the  game look a lot shinier  but having 3d portraits of each  character further develops the  connection you have  to not only your ruler but to the  characters you meet and add to your  court  and while it's all well and good seeing  everyone looking nice and pretty each  portrait is dynamic so they will change  depending on not only their age but if  they are afflicted by an illness or if  they are wounded in battle  their visage will also be influenced by  the genetics of their bloodline  in a much deeper way this time round and  although i've yet to see an unfaithful  spouse be exposed by the appearance of a  fair-haired child  i'm certainly working on it spouses are  now more useful in crusader kings 3  having their own council slot  and this can be better utilized in  managing your kingdom rather than it  being basically a marriage for political  gain or alliances  as a result it means that it is much  easier for you to feel attached to the  important figures in your campaign  and will more acutely feel that  heartbreak when they get assassinated  or die of gout as you continue to build  your character with more freedom  as well as more player driven direction  it becomes much  easier to craft the amazing stories the  crusader king series is known for the  bizarre events that appear through your  game or the narrative you craft around  yourself as the world you build  continues to grow alongside your story  and feel far more compelling as a result  sharing in twisted tales of intrigue  like catching my murderous spouse  realizing my child is my brother's and  then dying of stress  or having my new court physician trying  to cure my gouty foot  by cutting off my entire leg for me this  has always been my favorite part of  crusader kings  the opportunity to weave your own  narrative tapestry even if it ends up  being full of holes  is one of the major draws of crusader  kings and the third game aims to make it  easier  and deeper based on what i played the  increased focus on accessibility does  nothing to take away  from the complexity that this series is  known for but uses that accessibility to  hook you in  and get you started and utilizes new rpg  elements to make you want to keep  playing  i'm excited to get back to my throne and  dig into more of the game  when it eventually releases later this  year thank you so much for watching my  preview of crusader kings 3.  if you have any further questions feel  free to leave them in the comments or  tweet at me at a regular dave  and for more crusader kings 3 coverage  make sure you subscribe  to gamespot  you
 

